  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Elevated tide cycles persist due to Post-Tropical\nCyclone Josephine well off the coast, resulting in widespread \nminor coastal flooding continuing for back bay areas in Ocean \nCounty, NJ and Sussex County, DE.\n\nPost-Tropical Cyclone Josephine has pulled away from the region\nas it's now over the Canadian Maritimes. However elevated water\nlevels persist, mainly in the back bay areas of Ocean County, \nNJ and Sussex County, Delaware. The Coastal Flood Advisory for\nthese counties is in effect until 6 PM Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...More frost and freeze impacts expected tonight\nwhere the growing season continues.\n\nThe Freeze Warnings and Frost Advisories were allowed to expire\nat 9 AM. Temperatures this morning dropped to at or below\nfreezing in many areas across portions of eastern Pennsylvania\nand New Jersey. Given the widespread freezing temperatures that\noccurred, it has been determined the 2026 growing season has\nended for the following counties...\n\nIn Pennsylvania...Berks, Lehigh, Western Chester, Western\nMontgomery, and Upper Bucks counties.\n\nIn New Jersey...Morris, Hunterdon, and Somerset counties.\n\nNo additional frost/freeze headlines will be issued for these\ncounties until Spring 2027.\n\nThe synoptic pattern will not change much today with an upper-\nlevel ridge across the Midwest shifting eastward through the \nweekend. This will support strong surface high pressure shifting\neastward into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic through the \nweekend.\n\nA chilly and dry airmass will remain with this strong surface \nhigh, and as the high itself settles more overhead, forecast \nsoundings show surface winds decoupling again tonight. This \nwill set the stage for additional efficient radiational cooling\ntonight. The forecast lows for tonight will once again drop to\nthe upper 20s to mid 30s for much of the area (with the only \nexception along the immediate coastal strip) with a favorable \nsetup for frost and freeze. Another set of some frost and\npossibly freeze headlines will be issued at a later time today.",
